module ky-circuit-court-civil.civil

import ky-state-rules.{ ActionCommenced, JudgmentEntered, NoticeOfAppealFiled }

// Civil actions in the Kentucky circuit courts, from the applicant's
// (plaintiff's) side. Every period below is stated in a note, not counted:
// CR 6.01 excludes the day of the act, includes the last day unless it is a
// Saturday, Sunday or legal holiday, and excludes intermediate Saturdays,
// Sundays and legal holidays from a period of less than seven days; lit/core
// has no counting rule for it. The court may not extend the time for action
// under CR 50.02, 52.02, 59.02, 59.04, 59.05 or 60.02 except as those rules
// state (CR 6.02). The Civil Rules state no period within which the summons
// must be served; the limitations statutes that bear on it are not vendored.
// The earlier outline's Kentucky decisions (Pike v. George, 434 S.W.2d 626
// (Ky. 1968), on CR 12.02(f); Steelvest, Inc. v. Scansteel Service Center,
// Inc., 807 S.W.2d 476 (Ky. 1991), on summary judgment) are removed: no
// reporter text is vendored. Circuit court local rules are not vendored
// either.
start complaint-filed

state complaint-filed: "Complaint filed" by applicant cite "CR 3.01"
{
  note "A civil action is commenced by the filing of a complaint with the court and the issuance of a summons or warning order thereon in good faith (CR 3.01). Upon the filing of the complaint the clerk forthwith issues the summons and, at the initiating party's direction, mails it with the complaint by registered or certified mail, transfers it to a person authorized to serve it, or returns it to the party for service; service by mail is complete only upon delivery (CR 4.01(1)). The summons and complaint are served together (CR 4.04(1)). The filing fees of CR 3.02 are not modeled."
  edge-complaint-filed-served "Summons and complaint served" -> answer-due {
    event ActionCommenced()
    authority cite "CR 4.04"
  }
}

decision answer-due: "Answer, CR 12 motion, or default" by office cite "CR 12.01"
{
  note "A defendant serves an answer within 20 days after service of the summons; an answer to a cross-claim, and a reply to a counterclaim, are due within 20 days after service. Service of a CR 12 motion alters these periods unless the court fixes a different time: if the court denies the motion or postpones it to trial, the responsive pleading is served within ten days after entry of the order; if it grants a motion for a more definite statement, within 10 days after service of that statement (CR 12.01). Every defense is asserted in the responsive pleading, except that lack of subject-matter or personal jurisdiction, improper venue, insufficiency of process or of its service, failure to state a claim upon which relief can be granted, and failure to join a party under CR 19 may be made by motion before pleading (CR 12.02)."
  edge-answer-due-answer "Answer served" -> discovery { authority cite "CR 12.01" }
  edge-answer-due-motion "CR 12.02 or 12.03 motion" -> pleading-motion { authority cite "CR 12.02" }
  edge-answer-due-default "No answer: party has failed to plead or otherwise defend" -> default-application {
    authority cite "CR 55.01"
  }
}

decision pleading-motion: "CR 12.02(f) or 12.03 motion decided" by court cite "CR 12.02"
{
  note "On a motion asserting failure to state a claim upon which relief can be granted, matters outside the pleading presented to and not excluded by the court turn the motion into one for summary judgment under CR 56, and all parties have a reasonable opportunity to present material pertinent to it (CR 12.02). After the pleadings are closed, but within such time as not to delay the trial, any party may move for judgment on the pleadings, on the same terms (CR 12.03). If the court denies the motion, the responsive pleading is served within ten days after entry of the order (CR 12.01)."
  edge-pleading-motion-granted "Granted: claims dismissed" -> dismissed-merits {
    authority cite "CR 41.02(3)"
  }
  edge-pleading-motion-amend "Pleading amended" -> amended { authority cite "CR 15.01" }
  edge-pleading-motion-denied "Denied: answer within ten days after entry of the order" -> discovery {
    authority cite "CR 12.01"
  }
}

state amended: "Amended pleading" by applicant cite "CR 15.01"
{
  note "A party may amend its pleading once as a matter of course at any time before a responsive pleading is served or, if no responsive pleading is permitted and the action is not on the trial calendar, within 20 days after serving it; otherwise only by leave of court or the adverse party's written consent, and leave is freely given when justice so requires. A party pleads in response to an amended pleading within the time remaining for response to the original pleading or within 10 days after service of the amended pleading, whichever is longer, unless the court otherwise orders (CR 15.01)."
  edge-amended-tested "Amended pleading tested again" -> pleading-motion {
    authority cite "CR 12.02"
  }
}

terminal dismissed-merits: "Dismissed on the pleadings" outcome [loss, dismissal] cite "CR 41.02(3)"
{
  note "Outcome from the applicant's side. Unless the court's order otherwise specifies, a dismissal not provided for in CR 41, other than one for lack of jurisdiction, improper venue, want of prosecution under CR 77.02(2) or failure to join a party under CR 19, operates as an adjudication upon the merits (CR 41.02(3)). An appeal runs from the notation of service of the final judgment (RAP 3(A))."
}

state default-application: "Application for default judgment" by applicant cite "CR 55.01"
{
  note "When a party against whom affirmative relief is sought has failed to plead or otherwise defend, the party entitled to a judgment by default applies to the court. A party in default who has appeared is served with written notice of the application at least three days before the hearing on it; a motion for judgment against a party in default for failure to appear is accompanied by the attorney's certificate that the defaulting party has served no papers on the attorney. The court, without a jury unless one is demanded by a party entitled to it or required by statute or the Constitution, conducts the hearings needed to fix damages or establish an averment; a party in default for failure to appear waives trial by jury (CR 55.01)."
  edge-default-application-granted "Default judgment entered" -> default-judgment {
    authority cite "CR 55.01"
  }
  edge-default-application-set-aside "Defendant moves to set the default aside" -> set-aside {
    authority cite "CR 55.02"
  }
}

decision set-aside: "Set the default judgment aside?" by court cite "CR 55.02"
{
  note "For good cause shown the court may set aside a judgment by default in accordance with CR 60.02 (CR 55.02): on the grounds and within the times that rule states (see relief-motion)."
  edge-set-aside-granted "Set aside" -> discovery { authority cite "CR 55.02" }
  edge-set-aside-refused "Refused" -> default-judgment { authority cite "CR 55.02" }
}

terminal default-judgment: "Default judgment for applicant" outcome [win] cite "CR 55.01"
{
  note "Outcome from the applicant's side. Damages and costs are not stated in the vendored rules for this posture."
}

state discovery: "Discovery" by applicant cite "CR 26.02"
{
  note "Parties may obtain discovery regarding any matter, not privileged, relevant to the subject matter of the pending action; it is no ground for objection that the information sought will be inadmissible at trial if it appears reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence (CR 26.02(1)). Trial-preparation materials need a showing of substantial need and undue hardship (CR 26.02(3)); experts are discovered as CR 26.02(4) provides. If a party fails to obey an order to provide or permit discovery, the court may, among other orders, strike pleadings, stay proceedings, dismiss the action or render a default judgment, and requires the disobedient party or its attorney to pay the reasonable expenses caused unless the failure was substantially justified (CR 37.02(2), (3))."
  edge-discovery-sj "Summary judgment motion" -> summary-judgment { authority cite "CR 56.03" }
  edge-discovery-sanction "Order not obeyed: dismissal as a sanction" -> sanction-dismissal {
    authority cite "CR 37.02(2)"
  }
  edge-discovery-prosecution "No pretrial step for a year: dismissal after notice" -> prosecution-dismissal {
    authority cite "CR 77.02(2)"
  }
  edge-discovery-voluntary "Notice or stipulation of dismissal" -> voluntary-dismissal {
    authority cite "CR 41.01"
  }
  edge-discovery-trial "No dispositive motion" -> trial { authority cite "CR 38.02" }
}

terminal sanction-dismissal: "Dismissed as a discovery sanction" outcome [loss, dismissal] cite "CR 37.02(2)"
{
  note "Outcome from the applicant's side. The dismissal operates as an adjudication upon the merits unless the order otherwise specifies (CR 41.02(3))."
}

terminal prosecution-dismissal: "Dismissed for want of prosecution" outcome [loss, dismissal] cite "CR 77.02(2)"
{
  note "At least once a year the trial court reviews its pending actions; the attorneys of record in every case in which no pretrial step has been taken within the last year receive notice that the case will be dismissed in thirty days for want of prosecution except for good cause shown, and the court dismisses without prejudice each case in which no answer, or an insufficient answer, is made (CR 77.02(2)). A defendant may also move to dismiss for the plaintiff's failure to prosecute or to comply with the rules or an order (CR 41.02(1)); that dismissal operates as an adjudication upon the merits unless the order otherwise specifies (CR 41.02(3))."
}

terminal voluntary-dismissal: "Voluntary dismissal" outcome [settlement] cite "CR 41.01"
{
  note "Subject to CR 23.05, CR 66 and any statute, the plaintiff may dismiss without order of court by filing a notice of dismissal at any time before the adverse party serves an answer or a motion for summary judgment, whichever first occurs, or by filing a stipulation of dismissal signed by all parties who have appeared. Unless otherwise stated the dismissal is without prejudice, except that a notice of dismissal operates as an adjudication upon the merits when filed by a plaintiff who has once dismissed an action including the same claim in any court. Otherwise the action is dismissed at the plaintiff's instance only by court order, on terms the court deems proper (CR 41.01)."
}

decision summary-judgment: "Summary judgment decided" by court cite "CR 56.03"
{
  note "The motion is served at least 10 days before the time fixed for the hearing, and the adverse party may serve opposing affidavits before the day of the hearing. The judgment sought is rendered forthwith if the pleadings, depositions, answers to interrogatories, stipulations and admissions on file, with any affidavits, show that there is no genuine issue as to any material fact and that the moving party is entitled to a judgment as a matter of law; a summary judgment, interlocutory in character, may be rendered on liability alone although there is a genuine issue as to damages (CR 56.03)."
  edge-summary-judgment-granted-applicant "Granted for applicant" -> judgment-entered {
    event JudgmentEntered()
    authority cite "CR 56.03"
  }
  edge-summary-judgment-granted-defense "Granted for defendant" -> judgment-entered {
    event JudgmentEntered()
    authority cite "CR 56.03"
  }
  edge-summary-judgment-denied "Denied" -> trial { authority cite "CR 56.03" }
}

state trial: "Trial by jury or by the court" by court cite "CR 38.02"
{
  note "Any party may demand a trial by jury of any issue triable of right by a jury by serving a written demand at any time after commencement of the action and not later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue; the demand may be indorsed on a pleading, and if indorsed on the complaint its filing is service of the demand (CR 38.02). A party who moves for a directed verdict at the close of the opponent's evidence may offer evidence if the motion is not granted; the motion states its specific grounds (CR 50.01). In a bench trial the defendant may move for dismissal after the plaintiff has presented its evidence (CR 41.02(2))."
  edge-trial-judgment "Verdict or decision: judgment entered" -> judgment-entered {
    event JudgmentEntered()
    authority cite "CR 58"
  }
  edge-trial-voluntary "Settled before trial: stipulation of dismissal" -> voluntary-dismissal {
    authority cite "CR 41.01"
  }
}

decision judgment-entered: "Judgment entered and its service noted" by office cite "CR 58"
{
  note "A judgment or order is signed by the judge; the clerk forthwith notes it in the civil docket, and that notation is the entry, effective at the time of notation (CR 58(1)). Immediately upon entry of a judgment or final order the clerk serves notice of the entry by mail on every party not in default for failure to appear, and notes the service and its date in the docket; the date of that notation, or of an earlier filed waiver, is the date of entry for the running of the time for appeal under RAP 3 (CR 77.04(1), (2)). Where multiple claims or parties are involved, a judgment on fewer than all of them is final only on the court's determination, recited in it, that there is no just reason for delay and that it is final (CR 54.02(1)). Unless a statute or court rule provides a different time, the notice of appeal is filed with the circuit court clerk no later than 30 days from the date of notation of service; on excusable neglect based on a party's failure to learn of the entry, the trial court may extend the time by not more than 10 days (RAP 3(A), (D))."
  edge-judgment-entered-post-trial "CR 50.02, 52.02 or 59 motion served" -> post-trial {
    authority cite "CR 59.02"
  }
  edge-judgment-entered-appeal "Notice of appeal filed" -> appeal-taken {
    event NoticeOfAppealFiled()
    authority cite "RAP 2(A)"
  }
  edge-judgment-entered-relief "Motion for relief from judgment" -> relief-motion {
    authority cite "CR 60.02"
  }
  edge-judgment-entered-final "No appeal within time" -> final-no-appeal {
    authority cite "RAP 3(A)"
  }
}

decision post-trial: "Post-trial motions decided" by court cite "CR 59.02"
{
  note "Not later than 10 days after entry of judgment, a party who moved for a directed verdict at the close of all the evidence may move to set aside the verdict and judgment and have judgment entered in accordance with that motion, or, if no verdict was returned, within 10 days after the jury's discharge; a motion for a new trial may be joined with it (CR 50.02). A motion for a new trial on the grounds of CR 59.01 is served not later than 10 days after entry of the judgment (CR 59.02), and a motion to alter, amend or vacate a judgment not later than 10 days after entry of the final judgment (CR 59.05). If a party timely files a CR 50.02, 52.02 or 59 motion, the time to appeal runs for all parties from the entry of the order disposing of the last of them, except when a new trial is granted under CR 59; no motion under any other civil rule tolls it (RAP 3(E)(2))."
  edge-post-trial-new-trial "New trial granted" -> trial { authority cite "CR 59.01" }
  edge-post-trial-denied "Denied: judgment stands" -> judgment-entered {
    event JudgmentEntered()
    authority cite "RAP 3(E)"
  }
}

decision relief-motion: "Relief from judgment decided" by court cite "CR 60.02"
{
  note "On motion the court may relieve a party from its final judgment, order or proceeding for (a) mistake, inadvertence, surprise or excusable neglect; (b) newly discovered evidence that by due diligence could not have been discovered in time to move for a new trial under CR 59.02; (c) perjury or falsified evidence; (d) fraud affecting the proceedings; (e) a void, satisfied, released or discharged judgment, or one whose basis has been reversed or vacated, or whose prospective application is no longer equitable; or (f) any other reason of an extraordinary nature. The motion is made within a reasonable time, and on grounds (a), (b) and (c) not more than one year after the judgment, order or proceeding was entered or taken; it does not affect the finality of a judgment or suspend its operation (CR 60.02)."
  edge-relief-motion-granted "Relief granted: proceedings reopened" -> discovery {
    authority cite "CR 60.02"
  }
  edge-relief-motion-denied "Relief denied" -> final-no-appeal { authority cite "CR 60.02" }
}

terminal appeal-taken: "Appeal taken: continue in the Kentucky Court of Appeals package" outcome [appeal] cite "RAP 2(A)"
{
  note "All appeals are taken by filing a notice of appeal in the court from which the appeal is taken within the time allowed by RAP 3; civil appeals go to the next higher court. The timely filing of the notice is jurisdictional (RAP 2(A)(1), (2)). Hand-off to lit/ky-court-of-appeals (ky-court-of-appeals-civil)."
}

terminal final-no-appeal: "Final: no appeal" outcome [final] cite "RAP 3(A)"
{
  note "Outcome from the applicant's side. The failure of a party to file a notice of appeal timely results in dismissal (RAP 2(A)(3))."
}
